From: Gee <gehee@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] video: rockchip: mpp: rkvdec2: decode a picture as its slices arrive

A picture had to be in memory whole before the RK3568's decoder could start
on it, so over a link that sends each picture in slices, decoding began only
once the last slice was in. The decoder can do better, undocumented: in
stream mode (h26x_stream_mode set, h26x_stream_lastpacket clear) it raises
buf_empty when its data runs out, without an error, and waits; given the
next part as a new packet - RLC base at it, 16-byte aligned with the rest in
strm_start_bit, str_len its length, lastpacket on the last part, dec_e
written again with dec_e_rewrite_valid set - it goes on. Raising str_len
alone, not writing dec_e, or writing it without dec_e_rewrite_valid does
not wake it, nor does a part that ends at its last byte with no zero
padding after it. Tested on the air unit's 1080p100 stream: bit-exact,
deblocking and SAO across the slice boundary included, with waits of up to
10 ms between parts; the time from the last slice to the finished picture
halves (median 0.95 ms against 1.8 ms for the whole picture, on the
decoder; 2.0 ms against 5.1 ms through MPP).

An interrupt is a pause only when buf_empty is the one status bit set of
bits 2-9 (0x043 seen at a pause): with ready, cabac_end or colmv_ref_err as
well the decoder is done or failed, and a restart would set it going again.

MPP_CMD_STREAM_APPEND, in a range of its own (0x800), hands a session's
streamed picture its next part: an offset and length in its stream buffer,
and MPP_STREAM_LAST on the last. The decoder gets it at once if it is
waiting, or from the queue when it runs out. No data with MPP_STREAM_LAST
ends a picture whose rest is lost: it gets 64 zero bytes as its last part,
the end its data would have had - or, on a full queue, the part queued last
becomes the last. A part the picture cannot take, outside its buffer or with
no room in its queue, ends it the same way and comes back
MPP_STREAM_NO_PICTURE, as for a picture already ended. Busy or late is a
result, never an error: an error would leave the picture open, the decoder
waiting for a part that is not coming, and the request's msgs behind in
mpp_collect_msgs(), which now gives them back on any error. The appends name
no picture: they go to the session's newest streamed one, and one still open
when the next is sent is ended then. MPP_STREAM_PROBE asks whether the
device streams at all.

The decoder's own watchdog counts during a wait and tops out near 1 ms, so a
streamed task runs without it, and the driver keeps the watch.
rk_vcodec.rkvdec2_stream_wait_ms bounds a wait, then ends the picture the
same way. It is a safety net - the client ends a picture whose rest is lost
as soon as it knows - and has to outlast a key picture's second slice, which
can take over 250 ms over the radio: 400 ms by default, below the 500 ms
task timeout. rk_vcodec.rkvdec2_stream_stall_ms (50 ms, 0 for none) bounds
the decoding in between: a decoder that raises nothing for that long after it
was started or given a part is taken for hung, and its task ends as the task
timeout ends one - dec_timeout_sta, a reset - but now, not after 500 ms. A
whole 1080p picture decodes in under 4 ms. The waits stay under the task
timeout counted from the task's start as the driver records it:
mpp_task.on_run is only kept with timing_en on. A picture sent for streaming
to a decoder that cannot stream gets its last packet, and its watchdog, back.

MPP_STREAM_WAIT waits until the session's streamed picture has decoded the
data it has - its first slice is then in its output buffer, the second
still on its way - or is done, and says when it paused: a client can put
the picture on screen before the rest arrives. A signal ends the wait as its
timeout does. MPP_STREAM_PROBE answers MPP_STREAM_CAP_WAIT in length; a
kernel without it would have taken the wait for an append.

Link mode hands the decoder tasks from tables and cannot pause one, so with
rk_vcodec.rkvdec2_stream (on by default, read at probe) the RK3568 decoder
uses the single-task path. The link-mode node dump, run on IOMMU faults,
skips the tables that path does not have, and there dumps once a minute at
most: it busy-waits 28 ms in hard irq.

Signed-off-by: Gee <gehee@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 drivers/video/rockchip/mpp/mpp_common.c       |   3 +
 drivers/video/rockchip/mpp/mpp_rkvdec2.c      | 554 +++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 drivers/video/rockchip/mpp/mpp_rkvdec2.h      |  80 ++++
 drivers/video/rockchip/mpp/mpp_rkvdec2_link.c |  18 +-
 include/uapi/linux/rk-mpp.h                   |  49 +++
 5 files changed, 701 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/video/rockchip/mpp/mpp_common.c b/drivers/video/rockchip/mpp/mpp_common.c
index db93061..17476b6 100644
--- a/drivers/video/rockchip/mpp/mpp_common.c
+++ b/drivers/video/rockchip/mpp/mpp_common.c
@@ -1185,6 +1185,7 @@ static int mpp_check_cmd_v1(__u32 cmd)
 	found = (cmd >= MPP_CMD_SEND_BASE && cmd < MPP_CMD_SEND_BUTT) ? true : found;
 	found = (cmd >= MPP_CMD_POLL_BASE && cmd < MPP_CMD_POLL_BUTT) ? true : found;
 	found = (cmd >= MPP_CMD_CONTROL_BASE && cmd < MPP_CMD_CONTROL_BUTT) ? true : found;
+	found = (cmd >= MPP_CMD_STREAM_BASE && cmd < MPP_CMD_STREAM_BUTT) ? true : found;
 
 	return found ? 0 : -EINVAL;
 }
@@ -1619,6 +1620,8 @@ session_switch_done:
 	if (ret) {
 		mpp_err("session %d process cmd %x ret %d\n",
 			session->index, req->cmd, ret);
+		/* fpvOS: give the msgs back; each refused request kept one until close */
+		put_task_msgs(msgs);
 		return ret;
 	}
 
diff --git a/drivers/video/rockchip/mpp/mpp_rkvdec2.c b/drivers/video/rockchip/mpp/mpp_rkvdec2.c
index 50ec933..dbe0cce 100644
--- a/drivers/video/rockchip/mpp/mpp_rkvdec2.c
+++ b/drivers/video/rockchip/mpp/mpp_rkvdec2.c
@@ -391,6 +391,520 @@ void *rkvdec2_alloc_task(struct mpp_session *session,
 	return &task->mpp_task;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Stream mode (fpvOS). A picture can go to the decoder with only its first
+ * part, its first slice, in memory, and the rest is handed over while it
+ * decodes, by MPP_CMD_STREAM_APPEND: the decoder starts on the first slice
+ * while the next is still on its way. Nothing of this is documented by
+ * Rockchip; it was found on the RK3568 (vdpu34x) by experiment:
+ *
+ * - With h26x_stream_mode set and h26x_stream_lastpacket clear, a decoder
+ *   that runs out of data raises buf_empty (no error) and waits.
+ * - It goes on when given the next part as a new packet: RLC base at the
+ *   part (16-byte aligned, the rest of the way in strm_start_bit), str_len
+ *   its length, lastpacket set on the last part, and dec_e written again -
+ *   which it only takes with dec_e_rewrite_valid set. Raising str_len
+ *   alone, or not writing dec_e, does not wake it. A part should end in
+ *   zero padding, as MPP pads a whole picture: one that stops at its last
+ *   byte leaves the decoder waiting.
+ * - The result is bit-exact, deblocking and SAO across the slice boundary
+ *   included, with a wait of 10 ms between the parts.
+ * - Its own watchdog (dec_timeout_e) counts during the wait and has a
+ *   ceiling near 1 ms, so a stream task runs without it. The driver keeps
+ *   the watch instead, a timer for each state the decoder is in: waiting
+ *   for data, bounded by rkvdec2_stream_wait_ms, and decoding the data it
+ *   has, bounded by rkvdec2_stream_stall_ms.
+ *
+ * Link mode hands the decoder its tasks from tables and has no way to pause
+ * one, so streaming needs the single-task path: rkvdec2_stream (read at
+ * probe) turns the RK3568's link mode off.
+ */
+static bool rkvdec2_stream = true;
+module_param(rkvdec2_stream, bool, 0444);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(rkvdec2_stream, "RK3568: take pictures streamed slice by slice (turns link mode off)");
+
+/*
+ * A safety net: the client ends a picture whose rest is lost as soon as it
+ * knows (the next picture's first slice). It has to outlast the slowest part:
+ * a key picture's second slice can take over 250 ms to come over the radio.
+ * Below the 500 ms after which a task times out.
+ */
+static int rkvdec2_stream_wait_ms = 400;
+module_param(rkvdec2_stream_wait_ms, int, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(rkvdec2_stream_wait_ms, "how long a streamed picture waits for its next part before it is ended");
+
+/*
+ * The decoder's own watchdog, in software: how long it may work on the data
+ * it has without an interrupt - the end of that data, or of the picture -
+ * before it is taken for hung and reset, as its watchdog would have done
+ * within a millisecond. Without it a hang mid-picture held the decoder until
+ * the task's 500 ms timeout. A whole 1080p picture decodes in under 4 ms;
+ * the rest is room for a slow clock and a busy memory bus. 0 leaves it to
+ * the task timeout.
+ */
+static int rkvdec2_stream_stall_ms = 50;
+module_param(rkvdec2_stream_stall_ms, int, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(rkvdec2_stream_stall_ms, "how long a streamed picture may decode without an interrupt before it is taken for hung (0: off)");
+
+#define RKVDEC_STRM_ZERO_SIZE	SZ_4K
+#define RKVDEC_STRM_ZERO_LEN	64
+
+static bool rkvdec2_strm_capable(struct mpp_dev *mpp)
+{
+	struct rkvdec2_dev *dec = to_rkvdec2_dev(mpp);
+
+	return rkvdec2_stream && mpp->task_capacity == 1 && dec->strm_zero;
+}
+
+/* Under strm_lock: the decoder works on data it has; watch it for a hang. */
+static void rkvdec2_strm_watch(struct rkvdec2_dev *dec, struct rkvdec2_task *task)
+{
+	int ms = READ_ONCE(rkvdec2_stream_stall_ms);
+
+	if (ms <= 0)
+		return;
+	dec->strm_runner = task;
+	dec->strm_stall_due = ktime_add_ms(ktime_get(), ms);
+	hrtimer_start(&dec->strm_stall_timer, dec->strm_stall_due, HRTIMER_MODE_ABS_HARD);
+}
+
+/* Under strm_lock: the decoder stopped, or the task is gone. */
+static void rkvdec2_strm_unwatch(struct rkvdec2_dev *dec, struct rkvdec2_task *task)
+{
+	if (dec->strm_runner == task) {
+		dec->strm_runner = NULL;
+		/* never hrtimer_cancel() here: the callback takes this lock */
+		hrtimer_try_to_cancel(&dec->strm_stall_timer);
+	}
+}
+
+/* Under strm_lock, with the task on the decoder: give it the next part. */
+static void rkvdec2_strm_feed(struct mpp_dev *mpp, struct rkvdec2_task *task,
+			      const struct rkvdec2_strm_chunk *c)
+{
+	u32 skip = c->addr & 15;
+	u32 r15 = task->reg[RKVDEC_REG_STRM_PARAM_INDEX] & ~RKVDEC_STRM_START_BIT_MASK;
+	u32 r64 = task->reg[RKVDEC_REG_H26X_SET_INDEX];
+
+	mpp_write_relaxed(mpp, RKVDEC_REG_RLC_BASE, c->addr - skip);
+	mpp_write_relaxed(mpp, RKVDEC_REG_STRM_PARAM,
+			  r15 | ((skip * 8) << RKVDEC_STRM_START_BIT_SHIFT));
+	mpp_write_relaxed(mpp, RKVDEC_REG_STR_LEN, c->len + skip);
+	mpp_write_relaxed(mpp, RKVDEC_REG_H26X_SET,
+			  c->last ? r64 | RKVDEC_H26X_LASTPACKET : r64);
+	/* the registers before the restart */
+	wmb();
+	mpp_write(mpp, RKVDEC_REG_START_EN_BASE,
+		  task->reg[mpp->var->hw_info->reg_en] | RKVDEC_START_EN);
+	rkvdec2_strm_watch(to_rkvdec2_dev(mpp), task);
+	if (c->last)
+		task->strm_last = true;
+}
+
+/* Under strm_lock: stop the wait timer if it watches this task. */
+static void rkvdec2_strm_unwait(struct rkvdec2_dev *dec, struct rkvdec2_task *task)
+{
+	task->strm_waiting = false;
+	if (dec->strm_waiter == task) {
+		dec->strm_waiter = NULL;
+		/* never hrtimer_cancel() here: the callback takes this lock */
+		hrtimer_try_to_cancel(&dec->strm_timer);
+	}
+}
+
+/*
+ * Under strm_lock: hand the task a part - to the decoder if it waits for
+ * one, else to its queue. False if the queue is full.
+ */
+static bool rkvdec2_strm_give(struct mpp_dev *mpp, struct rkvdec2_task *task,
+			      const struct rkvdec2_strm_chunk *c)
+{
+	if (task->strm_waiting && mpp->cur_task == &task->mpp_task) {
+		/* it waits for exactly this */
+		rkvdec2_strm_unwait(to_rkvdec2_dev(mpp), task);
+		rkvdec2_strm_feed(mpp, task, c);
+	} else if (task->strm_count - task->strm_head < RKVDEC_STRM_CHUNKS) {
+		task->strm_q[task->strm_count++ % RKVDEC_STRM_CHUNKS] = *c;
+	} else {
+		return false;
+	}
+	if (c->last)
+		task->strm_closed = true;
+	return true;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Under strm_lock: the rest of the picture is lost - end it where its data
+ * ends. It gets 64 zero bytes as its last part, the end its data would have
+ * had; on a full queue the part queued last becomes the last instead, its
+ * own zero padding the end. An end always gets in: a picture left open would
+ * hold the decoder until its wait ran out.
+ */
+static void rkvdec2_strm_cut(struct mpp_dev *mpp, struct rkvdec2_task *task)
+{
+	struct rkvdec2_dev *dec = to_rkvdec2_dev(mpp);
+	struct rkvdec2_strm_chunk c = {
+		.addr = (u32)dec->strm_zero->iova,
+		.len = RKVDEC_STRM_ZERO_LEN,
+		.last = true,
+	};
+
+	dec->strm_lost++;
+	task->strm_closed = true;
+	/* full, so not empty; and a task waiting for data has an empty queue */
+	if (!rkvdec2_strm_give(mpp, task, &c))
+		task->strm_q[(task->strm_count - 1) % RKVDEC_STRM_CHUNKS].last = true;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Hard irq, a streamed picture's interrupt. Out of data and nothing else -
+ * of status bits 2-9 only buf_empty: not done, no error - is a pause: give
+ * the decoder the next part if one is queued, else let it wait for one.
+ * False for the end of the picture: done or failed, out of data after its
+ * last part, or already taken for hung.
+ */
+static bool rkvdec2_strm_irq(struct mpp_dev *mpp, struct rkvdec2_task *task, u32 status)
+{
+	struct rkvdec2_dev *dec = to_rkvdec2_dev(mpp);
+	unsigned long flags;
+	bool taken = true;
+
+	spin_lock_irqsave(&dec->strm_lock, flags);
+	/* it stopped: whatever comes next, it is not decoding what it had */
+	rkvdec2_strm_unwatch(dec, task);
+	if (task->strm_last || task->strm_hung) {
+		taken = false;
+	} else if ((status & RKVDEC_STRM_STA_MASK) != RKVDEC_BUF_EMPTY_STA) {
+		/*
+		 * Out of data but also done, or with an error: an end. A
+		 * restart would set a finished decoder going again. Kept for
+		 * the log when buf_empty came with a bit that is no error.
+		 */
+		if ((status & RKVDEC_BUF_EMPTY_STA) &&
+		    !(status & (RKVDEC_ERROR_STA | RKVDEC_TIMEOUT_STA | RKVDEC_BUS_STA)))
+			task->strm_end_sta = status;
+		taken = false;
+	} else if (task->strm_head < task->strm_count) {
+		rkvdec2_strm_feed(mpp, task, &task->strm_q[task->strm_head++ % RKVDEC_STRM_CHUNKS]);
+	} else {
+		task->strm_waiting = true;
+		dec->strm_waiter = task;
+		dec->strm_waits++;
+		if (!task->strm_paused) {
+			struct rkvdec2_session_priv *priv = task->mpp_task.session ?
+				task->mpp_task.session->priv : NULL;
+
+			task->strm_paused = true;
+			if (priv) {
+				priv->strm_pause_us = ktime_to_us(ktime_get());
+				priv->strm_paused_seq = task->strm_seq;
+				wake_up_all(&dec->strm_wq);
+			}
+		}
+		{
+			/* The wait restarts at each pause, but the task's own 500 ms
+			 * timeout runs from its start: end the picture before that,
+			 * whatever its pauses add up to. */
+			s64 ran = ktime_ms_delta(ktime_get(), task->strm_start);
+			s64 wait = min_t(s64, READ_ONCE(rkvdec2_stream_wait_ms), 450 - ran);
+
+			hrtimer_start(&dec->strm_timer, ms_to_ktime(max_t(s64, wait, 1)),
+				      HRTIMER_MODE_REL_HARD);
+		}
+	}
+	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&dec->strm_lock, flags);
+
+	return taken;
+}
+
+/* The wait ran out: end the picture where its data ends. */
+static enum hrtimer_restart rkvdec2_strm_timer(struct hrtimer *timer)
+{
+	struct rkvdec2_dev *dec = container_of(timer, struct rkvdec2_dev, strm_timer);
+	struct rkvdec2_task *task;
+	unsigned long flags;
+
+	spin_lock_irqsave(&dec->strm_lock, flags);
+	task = dec->strm_waiter;
+	if (task && task->strm_waiting && dec->mpp.cur_task == &task->mpp_task) {
+		task->strm_timed_out = true;
+		rkvdec2_strm_cut(&dec->mpp, task);
+	}
+	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&dec->strm_lock, flags);
+
+	return HRTIMER_NORESTART;
+}
+
+/*
+ * The decoder had data for rkvdec2_stream_stall_ms and raised nothing: it
+ * hangs. Handled as an interrupt of its own watchdog would be, now instead
+ * of at the task's 500 ms timeout: the task ends with dec_timeout_sta, the
+ * way mpp_task_timeout_work() ends one, and the decoder is reset.
+ */
+static enum hrtimer_restart rkvdec2_strm_stall(struct hrtimer *timer)
+{
+	struct rkvdec2_dev *dec = container_of(timer, struct rkvdec2_dev, strm_stall_timer);
+	struct mpp_dev *mpp = &dec->mpp;
+	struct rkvdec2_task *task;
+	struct mpp_task *mpp_task;
+	unsigned long flags;
+
+	spin_lock_irqsave(&dec->strm_lock, flags);
+	task = dec->strm_runner;
+	/* watched again while this waited for the lock: not due yet */
+	if (!task || ktime_before(ktime_get(), dec->strm_stall_due))
+		goto unlock;
+	dec->strm_runner = NULL;
+	mpp_task = &task->mpp_task;
+	/* unless an interrupt or the task timeout is handling it already */
+	if (mpp->cur_task != mpp_task ||
+	    test_and_set_bit(TASK_STATE_HANDLE, &mpp_task->state))
+		goto unlock;
+	task->strm_hung = true;
+	dec->strm_hangs++;
+	cancel_delayed_work(&mpp_task->timeout_work);
+	mpp->irq_status = RKVDEC_TIMEOUT_STA | RKVDEC_IRQ_RAW | RKVDEC_IRQ;
+	set_bit(TASK_STATE_TIMEOUT, &mpp_task->state);
+	kthread_queue_work(&mpp->queue->worker, &mpp->work);
+unlock:
+	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&dec->strm_lock, flags);
+
+	return HRTIMER_NORESTART;
+}
+
+/* A task being made: is it a streamed picture? Then it takes appends. */
+static void rkvdec2_strm_open(struct mpp_session *session, struct rkvdec2_task *task)
+{
+	struct mpp_dev *mpp = session->mpp;
+	struct rkvdec2_dev *dec = to_rkvdec2_dev(mpp);
+	struct rkvdec2_session_priv *priv = session->priv;
+	struct mpp_task *mpp_task = &task->mpp_task;
+	u32 r64 = task->reg[RKVDEC_REG_H26X_SET_INDEX];
+	struct rkvdec2_task *prev;
+	unsigned long flags;
+	u32 i;
+
+	if (!(r64 & RKVDEC_H26X_STREAM_MODE) || (r64 & RKVDEC_H26X_LASTPACKET) || !priv)
+		return;
+	if (!rkvdec2_strm_capable(mpp)) {
+		/* nothing more will come: decode what there is, watched as any */
+		task->reg[RKVDEC_REG_H26X_SET_INDEX] |= RKVDEC_H26X_LASTPACKET;
+		task->reg[RKVDEC_REG_IMPORTANT_INDEX] |= RKVDEC_DEC_TIMEOUT_E;
+		return;
+	}
+	for (i = 0; i < mpp_task->mem_count; i++) {
+		struct mpp_mem_region *r = &mpp_task->mem_regions[i];
+
+		if (r->reg_idx == RKVDEC_REG_RLC_BASE_INDEX) {
+			task->strm_buf = (u32)r->iova;
+			task->strm_buf_len = r->len;
+			break;
+		}
+	}
+	if (!task->strm_buf_len) {
+		task->reg[RKVDEC_REG_H26X_SET_INDEX] |= RKVDEC_H26X_LASTPACKET;
+		task->reg[RKVDEC_REG_IMPORTANT_INDEX] |= RKVDEC_DEC_TIMEOUT_E;
+		return;
+	}
+
+	/* its watchdog counts while it waits; the driver's timers watch it instead */
+	task->reg[RKVDEC_REG_IMPORTANT_INDEX] &= ~RKVDEC_DEC_TIMEOUT_E;
+	/* the pause, and the restart that ends it */
+	task->reg[RKVDEC_REG_IMPORTANT_INDEX] |= RKVDEC_BUF_EMPTY_EN | RKVDEC_DEC_E_REWRITE_VALID;
+	task->strm = true;
+
+	spin_lock_irqsave(&dec->strm_lock, flags);
+	/*
+	 * The session's appends go to its newest streamed picture: they name
+	 * none. One still open gets no more, so it ends here, rather than hold
+	 * the decoder until its wait runs out.
+	 */
+	prev = priv->strm_task;
+	if (prev && !prev->strm_closed)
+		rkvdec2_strm_cut(mpp, prev);
+	priv->strm_task = task;
+	task->strm_seq = ++priv->strm_seq;
+	if (!dec->strm_pictures++)
+		dev_info(mpp->dev, "decoding pictures as their slices arrive (stream mode)\n");
+	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&dec->strm_lock, flags);
+}
+
+/* The task goes on the decoder with its first part: its timers start now. */
+static void rkvdec2_strm_run(struct mpp_dev *mpp, struct rkvdec2_task *task)
+{
+	struct rkvdec2_dev *dec = to_rkvdec2_dev(mpp);
+	unsigned long flags;
+
+	spin_lock_irqsave(&dec->strm_lock, flags);
+	/* not mpp_task.on_run: that one is only kept while timing_en is on */
+	task->strm_start = ktime_get();
+	rkvdec2_strm_watch(dec, task);
+	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&dec->strm_lock, flags);
+}
+
+/* A task done or dropped: it takes no more appends. */
+static void rkvdec2_strm_close(struct mpp_session *session, struct rkvdec2_task *task)
+{
+	struct rkvdec2_dev *dec;
+	struct rkvdec2_session_priv *priv;
+	unsigned long flags;
+
+	if (!task->strm || !session || !session->mpp)
+		return;
+	dec = to_rkvdec2_dev(session->mpp);
+	priv = session->priv;
+
+	spin_lock_irqsave(&dec->strm_lock, flags);
+	task->strm_closed = true;
+	rkvdec2_strm_unwait(dec, task);
+	rkvdec2_strm_unwatch(dec, task);
+	if (priv && priv->strm_task == task)
+		priv->strm_task = NULL;
+	if (priv && (s32)(task->strm_seq - priv->strm_done_seq) > 0)
+		priv->strm_done_seq = task->strm_seq;
+	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&dec->strm_lock, flags);
